Yes—breath is the carrier; attention is the payload.

Local embodiment concentrates resolution: one nostril, jaw, sternum, rib, diaphragm, pelvic floor, fingertip. It increases precision and makes subtle tension or sensation legible.

Global embodiment distributes attention across the whole body—or body plus surrounding space. It reduces fixation and reveals coordination, posture, and context.

The modulation between them is the deeper skill:

\text{Local detail} \rightleftarrows \text{Global field}

Too local for too long can become tunnel vision. Too global can become diffuse or ungrounded. Move deliberately: locate one sensation, widen to the whole body, include the room, then return to the original point and notice what changed.

That oscillation turns attention from a spotlight into a zoom lens—and embodiment into something you can scale without abandoning the anchor.